1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:05,648
Why did ancient Indian builders carve the world's largest monolithic temple backwards?

2
00:00:05,598 --> 00:00:09,557
At Kailasa, they didn't start with the foundations.

3
00:00:09,557 --> 00:00:15,533
They carved the roof first, then the ceiling, then the pillars, and finally the floor!

4
00:00:15,533 --> 00:00:16,927
Why?

5
00:00:16,927 --> 00:00:19,583
Because there was no scaffolding!

6
00:00:19,583 --> 00:00:22,864
The temple sits in a 100-foot abyss.

7
00:00:22,864 --> 00:00:28,789
If they tried building from the ground up, they would need millions of tons of wooden scaffolding!

8
00:00:28,789 --> 00:00:33,893
By carving from the top down, the mountain itself became their scaffolding!

9
00:00:33,893 --> 00:00:39,257
As workers chiseled downward, they stood on the very rock they were about to carve!

10
00:00:39,257 --> 00:00:43,854
One mistake, and the entire multi-ton roof would collapse.

11
00:00:43,854 --> 00:00:50,260
See how master architect Kokasa hollowed the sanctum ceiling in Episode Three linked below!

